    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d309">

                <pb facs="0483" n="465" xml:id="pg465"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.00/6–2246: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-22T18:00:00+01:00">June 22, 1946—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3050. Referring to Bidault’s efforts to form a government and especially to the
                    Communist attitude thereto, several prominent Frenchmen have remarked recently
                    that the only card of value that Bidault holds in his game with the Communists
                    is the fact that the Communists are apprehensive lest Bidault’s failure to form
                    a government might produce such chaotic political conditions here that De Gaulle
                    might return to power. The Communists are not afraid of Bidault but are very
                    much afraid of De Gaulle.</p>
                <p>Repeated London 463, Moscow 246; Berlin 250.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[On July 11 the Acting Secretary of State instructed the United States
                        consular officers at Algiers, Casablanca, Rabat, and Tunis to begin
                        reporting regularly on Communist propaganda and other activities in French
                        North Africa. For documentation on this subject, see volume VII, “Interest
                        of the United States in Communist and Nationalist Activities in North
                        Africa.”]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
